  "enhanced_information": "Hepatic hemangiomas are benign vascular tumors that are usually asymptomatic and do not require surgical intervention unless they cause symptoms or complications. Giant hemangiomas can be associated with Kasabach-Merritt syndrome, which leads to thrombocytopenia and consumption coagulopathy. Surgical resection is not routinely indicated based solely on size (greater than 3 cm) without symptoms. Percutaneous embolization is not the first-line treatment; surgical resection is preferred for symptomatic cases. Biopsy is generally unnecessary due to the characteristic imaging features of hemangiomas, which can be effectively diagnosed through MRI. MRI is valuable for differentiating hemangiomas from other liver lesions, confirming its role in diagnostic characterization."
